"""Content data: aws-clf (part 1 of 2) — start-here, compute-deep, database-services, mock, capstone, labs 1-4."""
from cbt_builder import L, S, LB, D, start_here_domain, mock_domain, capstone_domain
from cbt_data_awsclf2 import build_awsclf2
CN = 'AWS Cloud Practitioner'

def build_awsclf():
    new_domains = []
    labs = []
    new_domains.append(start_here_domain(CN, 'CLF-C02 exam', 'Cloud Concepts'))

    def std(did, name, desc, lesson_specs, scen_specs, icon=None):
        lessons = [L(did, *sp) for sp in lesson_specs]
        scenarios = [S(did, *sp) for sp in scen_specs]
        new_domains.append(D(did, name, desc, lessons, scenarios, icon=icon))
    did = 'compute-deep'
    (std(did, 'Compute Deep Dive', 'EC2, Lambda, containers, and Elastic Beanstalk: choosing the right compute and pricing model for the CLF-C02 exam.', [('EC2: Virtual Servers in the Cloud', ['EC2 provides resizable virtual servers: you choose the instance type (compute, memory, storage optimized), the AMI (OS image), and the network placement', 'You manage the OS and above: patching the guest OS, installing applications, and configuring the instance firewall (security groups) are YOUR responsibility', "Instance families encode tradeoffs: C (compute), M (balanced), R/X (memory), P/G (GPU), I/D (storage) — match the family to the workload's bottleneck", 'EC2 is zonal: an instance lives in one Availability Zone; multi-AZ architectures need multiple instances behind a load balancer'], 'Launch checklist:\n  AMI: Amazon Linux 2023\n  Type: t3.medium (burstable, general purpose)\n  AZ: us-east-1a (with a twin in 1b for HA)\n  Security group: 443 in from ALB only, SSH from bastion\n  Storage: gp3 EBS, encrypted\n\nEvery choice maps to an exam concept.', "EC2's flexibility is its exam trap: questions test whether you know what YOU manage versus AWS. On EC2, the OS, patches, and app security are always yours — that never changes regardless of instance type.", ['Assuming AWS patches your EC2 guest OS — it does not', "One instance in one AZ called 'highly available'", 'Picking instance types by vCPU alone — family matters more'], 'For a web app needing 99.99% uptime, sketch the EC2 architecture: AZs, load balancer, instance count.'), ('EC2 Pricing Models', ['On-Demand: pay per second, no commitment — for spiky, unpredictable, or short-lived workloads where flexibility beats discount', 'Reserved Instances and Savings Plans: 1- or 3-year commitments for up to 72% off — for steady-state workloads you can predict; Savings Plans are the flexible modern form', 'Spot Instances: bid on spare capacity for up to 90% off — for fault-tolerant, interruptible work (batch, CI) that survives 2-minute interruption notices', 'Dedicated Hosts/Instances: single-tenant hardware — for compliance or licensing (BYOL) needs, at a premium over shared tenancy'], 'Workload -> pricing:\n  Web fleet, steady 24/7 -> Savings Plan (3-yr, ~60% off)\n  Nightly batch renders -> Spot (up to 90% off)\n  Month-end close, 3 days -> On-Demand\n  Oracle DB, license tied to cores -> Dedicated Host\n\nMatch commitment to predictability.', "The exam loves the 'which pricing model' question: the discriminator is always workload predictability + interruptibility. Steady = commit; spiky = On-Demand; interruptible = Spot; compliance = Dedicated.", ['Spot for a production database — interruption = outage', 'On-Demand for a flat 24/7 workload — paying 2-3x for no reason', 'Confusing Reserved Instances with Savings Plans flexibility'], 'Classify four workloads you know: which EC2 pricing model each, and why?'), ('Lambda: Serverless Functions', ['Lambda runs code without servers: upload the function, AWS handles provisioning, scaling, and patching — you pay per request plus GB-seconds of execution', 'Event-driven by design: triggers from S3 uploads, API Gateway, DynamoDB streams, schedules — Lambda shines at glue logic between services', '15-minute maximum execution: Lambda is for short tasks, not long batch jobs — and cold starts add latency to infrequent invocations', 'Scales automatically but not infinitely: concurrency limits and downstream throttling (e.g., a database) are the real-world constraints'], 'Lambda fit test:\n  Thumbnail on S3 upload -> YES (event, <1s)\n  REST API backend -> YES (API Gateway trigger)\n  6-hour video transcode -> NO (15-min limit; use Batch/Fargate)\n  Cron report, 2 min -> YES (EventBridge schedule)\n\nDuration decides.', "Lambda's exam angle is always the tradeoff: no server management and automatic scaling versus execution limits, cold starts, and pay-per-invocation math that punishes constant high-volume workloads (where EC2 or Fargate wins).", ['Lambda for long-running jobs — the 15-minute wall', 'Ignoring cold starts on latency-sensitive APIs', 'Forgetting downstream limits — Lambda scales, your database may not'], 'Name one workload perfect for Lambda and one terrible for it. What decides?'), ('Containers: ECS, EKS, and Fargate', ['Containers package app + dependencies: consistent from laptop to cloud — Docker images run on ECS (AWS-native orchestrator) or EKS (managed Kubernetes)', 'ECS vs EKS: ECS is simpler and AWS-integrated; EKS is Kubernetes-standard for teams with K8s skills or multi-cloud portability needs', 'Fargate removes the servers: run containers without managing EC2 instances — pay per vCPU/memory-second; the serverless option for containers', 'EC2 launch type vs Fargate: EC2 gives control (and management burden); Fargate gives simplicity at a price premium for constant workloads'], 'Choice map:\n  Team knows K8s, needs portability -> EKS\n  AWS-native microservices, simple ops -> ECS + Fargate\n  Constant high utilization, cost focus -> ECS on EC2\n  Batch container jobs -> Fargate (no idle servers)\n\nSkills + utilization decide.', 'Exam questions test the managed-vs-self-managed line: EKS manages the control plane (you still manage worker nodes unless Fargate); ECS + Fargate manages everything. Know exactly which layer AWS owns in each option.', ['EKS chosen with no Kubernetes expertise on the team', 'Fargate for 24/7 maxed-out containers — EC2 launch type is cheaper', 'Thinking containers remove all server management (EC2 launch type does not)'], 'Your team ships microservices, no K8s skills, spiky traffic: ECS, EKS, or Fargate? Why?'), ('Elastic Beanstalk and Lightsail', ['Elastic Beanstalk is PaaS: upload code, AWS provisions and manages the EC2, load balancer, and database plumbing — fastest path from code to running app', 'You keep full control underneath: Beanstalk environments expose the EC2 instances and settings — unlike pure serverless, you can SSH in and tune', 'Lightsail is simplified VPS: flat-rate virtual servers, databases, and storage for simple projects — predictable pricing, limited scaling', "Exam positioning: Beanstalk = 'deploy code quickly without managing infrastructure'; Lightsail = 'simple, predictable, small-scale'"], "PaaS decision:\n  'Just run my code, handle the infra' -> Beanstalk\n  'Simple VPS, fixed $5/mo budget' -> Lightsail\n  'I need container orchestration' -> ECS\n  'Event-driven micro-billing' -> Lambda\n\nAbstraction level matches the ask.", "Do not confuse Beanstalk with Lambda: Beanstalk runs long-lived servers (your code on EC2 it manages); Lambda runs short functions. 'Upload code and forget servers' describes both — the difference is duration and control.", ['Beanstalk for event-driven short tasks — Lambda fits better', 'Lightsail for auto-scaling enterprise workloads — it does not scale that way', 'Thinking Beanstalk locks you out of the infrastructure'], 'A startup needs a blog + small store, fixed budget, no ops team: Beanstalk or Lightsail?')], [('clf-compute-ec2resp', 'Who Patches the Server?', 'Your company runs its app on EC2. A critical Linux kernel vulnerability is announced. The CTO asks who is responsible for patching.', 'Per the shared responsibility model, who patches the EC2 guest OS?', ["The customer: on EC2, AWS manages the hypervisor and physical infrastructure, but the guest OS, applications, and data are the customer's responsibility.", 'AWS: they manage everything below the application.', 'It depends on the EC2 pricing model.', 'Nobody — EC2 auto-patches.'], 0, 'The correct answer is the shared-responsibility line for EC2: AWS = security OF the cloud (facilities, hardware, hypervisor); customer = security IN the cloud, which on EC2 includes the guest OS and up. The managed services (RDS, Lambda) shift more upward to AWS — EC2 shifts the least.', 'What does AWS manage on EC2 versus what do you manage?', ['Apply shared responsibility to EC2', 'Distinguish OF vs IN the cloud', 'Identify customer patching duties'], 10, [['Finding', 'Critical kernel CVE; fleet of 40 EC2 instances; no patch process']]), ('clf-compute-spot', 'The Batch Bargain', 'Your team runs nightly video renders: 6 hours, fault-tolerant, deadline 6 AM, currently on On-Demand at $400/night. Finance wants the bill cut.', 'What is the best pricing move?', ['Move to Lambda.', 'Spot Instances: the workload is interruptible and deadline-flexible — checkpointing handles the 2-minute interruption notice, and discounts up to 90% crush the bill.', 'Reserved Instances for 3 years.', 'Dedicated Hosts for compliance.'], 1, "The correct answer matches pricing to workload shape: interruptible + flexible deadline = Spot's ideal profile. Reserved Instances demand steady 24/7 usage; Lambda hits the 15-minute wall on 6-hour renders.", 'Which pricing model rewards interruptibility?', ['Match pricing to workload shape', 'Evaluate Spot suitability', 'Avoid over-committing'], 10, [['Workload', '6-hour nightly renders, checkpointed, deadline 6 AM, $400/night On-Demand']]), ('clf-compute-lambda15', 'The 45-Minute Function', 'A developer proposes a Lambda function that processes uploaded videos, averaging 45 minutes per file, triggered by S3 uploads.', 'What is wrong with this design?', ['Lambda is too expensive for video.', "Lambda's 15-minute maximum execution kills it: 45-minute videos exceed the hard limit. Use AWS Batch, Fargate, or EC2 for long processing — Lambda fits the short glue tasks around it.", 'Nothing — Lambda scales automatically.', 'S3 cannot trigger Lambda.'], 2, 'The correct answer names the hard limit: Lambda caps at 15 minutes per invocation, so a 45-minute job cannot complete. The exam tests this boundary constantly — duration is the first Lambda suitability check.', "What is Lambda's maximum execution time?", ['Know Lambda limits', 'Choose compute by duration', 'Design event-driven pipelines'], 10, [['Design', 'S3 trigger -> Lambda, 45-min average processing per video']]), ('clf-compute-beanstalk', 'Ship It by Friday', 'A two-person startup must deploy a Node.js app by Friday. No ops experience, no time to configure load balancers or auto-scaling.', 'Which service fits best?', ['EC2 with manual configuration.', 'EKS with Fargate.', 'AWS Outposts.', "Elastic Beanstalk: upload the code and AWS provisions the EC2 fleet, load balancer, and scaling — the PaaS option for 'just run my code.'"], 3, 'The correct answer is the highest-abstraction fit: Beanstalk exists for exactly this — code in, running app out, with the underlying infrastructure still accessible later. EKS and manual EC2 demand the ops skills the team lacks.', 'Which service minimizes operational burden for code deployment?', ['Choose PaaS for speed', 'Match abstraction to team skills', 'Identify Beanstalk use cases'], 10, [['Constraints', '2-person team, Node.js app, Friday deadline, zero ops experience']]), ('clf-compute-fargate', 'The K8s Question', 'Your team runs microservices on Kubernetes on-premises and wants the same APIs in AWS without managing the control plane. Some workloads are spiky.', 'What is the best fit?', ['EKS with Fargate: managed Kubernetes control plane (AWS handles masters) plus serverless workers for the spiky workloads — same K8s APIs, minimal node management.', 'ECS with EC2 launch type.', 'Lambda for everything.', 'Elastic Beanstalk.'], 0, "The correct answer preserves the team's K8s investment (EKS = managed Kubernetes) while Fargate removes worker-node management for spiky traffic. ECS would force re-platforming off Kubernetes APIs.", 'Which option keeps Kubernetes APIs with managed infrastructure?', ['Choose EKS for K8s portability', 'Apply Fargate for spiky containers', 'Preserve team skill investments'], 10, [['Team', 'K8s expertise on-prem; spiky microservice traffic; no desire to manage masters']]), ('clf-compute-az', 'The Single-AZ Outage', "A company's entire web tier runs on EC2 instances in a single Availability Zone. An AZ power event takes the site down for 4 hours.", 'What architecture prevents recurrence?', ['A second region for everything.', "Multi-AZ deployment: instances across at least two AZs behind an Elastic Load Balancer — AZs are isolated failure domains, and the region survives one AZ's failure.", 'Bigger instances in the same AZ.', 'More frequent backups.'], 1, 'The correct answer uses AZs as designed: each AZ is an isolated data-center cluster, and distributing across them with a load balancer survives single-AZ events. A second region is overkill for an AZ-level failure.', "What is an Availability Zone's failure-isolation purpose?", ['Design multi-AZ architectures', 'Use ELB across AZs', 'Right-size resilience to the failure'], 15, [['Incident', '4-hour outage; all instances in us-east-1a; post-mortem required']]), ('clf-compute-ri', 'The Steady Bill', 'A company runs 100 EC2 instances 24/7 for its core platform — flat usage for 18 months. They pay full On-Demand rates.', 'What is the cost-optimization move?', ['Switch to Lambda.', 'Turn instances off at night.', 'Savings Plans (or Reserved Instances): steady 24/7 usage is the textbook commitment case — up to 72% off for a 1- or 3-year commitment, with Savings Plans offering flexibility across instance families.', 'Move everything to Spot.'], 2, 'The correct answer commits where usage is predictable: 18 months of flat 24/7 demand will not surprise you, so the commitment discount is nearly free money. Spot risks interruption on the core platform; night shutdowns contradict 24/7.', 'When do commitment discounts pay off?', ['Apply Savings Plans to steady workloads', 'Quantify commitment savings', 'Avoid Spot for core steady state'], 10, [['Usage', '100 instances, 24/7, flat 18 months, full On-Demand pricing']]), ('clf-compute-lightsail', 'The Predictable Blog', "A freelancer needs to host 5 small WordPress sites. Budget is fixed, traffic is low, and AWS's full console feels overwhelming.", 'What fits best?', ['EC2 with Savings Plans.', 'EKS cluster.', 'Aurora Global Database.', 'Lightsail: flat-rate VPS bundles with predictable monthly pricing and a simplified console — designed for exactly this scale.'], 3, "The correct answer matches scale to service: Lightsail's fixed-price simplicity beats EC2's power (and complexity) for five low-traffic sites. Enterprise services would be expensive overkill.", 'Which service targets simple, fixed-price, small-scale hosting?', ['Identify Lightsail use cases', 'Match service to scale', 'Prefer simplicity at small scale'], 10, [['Needs', '5 WordPress sites, fixed budget, low traffic, minimal AWS experience']])], icon='💻'),)
    did = 'database-services'
    (std(did, 'Database Services', 'RDS, Aurora, DynamoDB, and the purpose-built databases: picking the right store for the CLF-C02 exam.', [('RDS: Managed Relational Databases', ['RDS runs MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, and SQL Server without the undifferentiated labor: AWS patches the OS and DB, backs up automatically, and handles failover', 'Multi-AZ for availability: a standby replica in another AZ with automatic failover — for production uptime, not performance', 'Read Replicas for scale: async copies that absorb read traffic (and can promote) — for performance, not failover speed', 'You still own the data and access: schema design, query tuning, user credentials, and encryption choices remain yours'], 'RDS decision:\n  Need HA for the orders DB? -> Multi-AZ\n  Reads overwhelming the primary? -> Read Replicas (up to 5)\n  Engine choice? -> workload + license (Postgres/MySQL = open)\n  Backups? -> automated, 7-day default retention\n\nHA and scale are separate knobs.', "The exam's favorite RDS trap is Multi-AZ vs Read Replicas: Multi-AZ is a standby you cannot read from (availability); Read Replicas serve reads (performance). Mixing them up fails the question every time.", ['Multi-AZ for read scaling — the standby takes no traffic', 'Read Replicas for fast failover — they are async and laggy', 'Assuming RDS tunes your queries — performance is still yours'], 'An RDS primary is read-saturated at peak: Multi-AZ, Read Replica, or bigger instance? Why?'), ('Aurora: Cloud-Native Relational', ["Aurora is AWS's cloud-native relational engine: MySQL- and PostgreSQL-compatible with up to 5x/3x the throughput of stock engines", 'Storage auto-scales to 128 TB and replicates 6 ways across 3 AZs — storage-level high availability without standby instances', "Up to 15 low-latency read replicas with millisecond failover priority tiers — read scaling far beyond RDS's 5", 'Aurora Global Database spans regions with <1s replication lag — for global apps needing low-latency reads worldwide'], 'Aurora vs RDS:\n  Highest perf + auto-scaling storage -> Aurora\n  Oracle/SQL Server needed -> RDS (Aurora lacks them)\n  Global reads, <1s lag -> Aurora Global\n  Cheapest simple MySQL -> RDS MySQL\n\nAurora wins on scale; RDS on engine breadth.', "Remember the compatibility story: Aurora speaks MySQL and PostgreSQL wire protocols, so migrations are often driver-level changes — but it is NOT bit-identical, so test. The exam tests 'which engine options' frequently.", ['Aurora for Oracle workloads — unsupported engine', 'Assuming 6-way storage replication means no backups needed', 'Choosing RDS when the workload needs 15 read replicas'], 'A global SaaS needs Postgres compatibility and fast regional reads: Aurora or RDS?'), ('DynamoDB: Serverless NoSQL', ['DynamoDB is serverless key-value/document NoSQL: single-digit millisecond latency at any scale, no servers to manage', 'Two capacity modes: Provisioned (you set read/write units — cheaper for predictable traffic) and On-Demand (pay per request — for spiky or new workloads)', 'DAX adds in-memory caching for microsecond reads; Global Tables replicate multi-region active-active for global apps', 'Design is access-pattern-first: model your partition keys around your queries — DynamoDB punishes relational thinking (no joins, no ad-hoc SQL)'], 'DynamoDB fit:\n  Session store, ms latency -> YES\n  Spiky new app, unknown traffic -> On-Demand\n  Steady 10k reads/s -> Provisioned (cheaper)\n  Complex reporting queries -> NO (use RDS/Redshift)\n\nKeys first, queries second.', "The exam tests DynamoDB's boundaries: it is not a relational database and not a data warehouse. 'Needs joins and complex queries' always points away from DynamoDB — usually to RDS or Redshift.", ['Using DynamoDB for analytical reporting — wrong tool', 'On-Demand for flat predictable traffic — overpaying', 'Relational schema design ported directly — hot partitions'], 'Shopping cart sessions, 50k writes/sec, global users: DynamoDB mode and features?'), ('Purpose-Built: ElastiCache, Redshift, Neptune, DocumentDB', ['ElastiCache: managed Redis/Memcached in-memory caching — microsecond reads in front of RDS/DynamoDB; Redis adds pub/sub and persistence', 'Redshift: petabyte-scale data warehouse for OLAP analytics — columnar storage, massively parallel queries; NOT for transactional workloads', 'Neptune: managed graph database — fraud detection, knowledge graphs, relationship-heavy queries that SQL struggles with', 'DocumentDB: MongoDB-compatible document store — for teams with MongoDB codebases wanting a managed AWS home'], "Database picker:\n  Cache hot reads -> ElastiCache (Redis)\n  Petabyte analytics -> Redshift\n  'Friends of friends' queries -> Neptune\n  Existing MongoDB app -> DocumentDB\n  Transactions + joins -> RDS/Aurora\n\nOne workload, one purpose-built store.", "CLF-C02 loves the purpose-built lineup: each service maps to a workload adjective — in-memory, warehouse-scale, graph, document. Learn the one-line 'when to use' for each and the exam's database questions become free points.", ['Redshift for order transactions — OLTP on an OLAP store', 'ElastiCache as the system of record — cache is not storage', 'Neptune for simple key-value — DynamoDB is cheaper and simpler'], 'Match four workloads (leaderboard cache, sales analytics, fraud graph, mobile backend) to services.'), ('DMS and Database Migration', ['AWS DMS migrates databases with minimal downtime: homogeneous (Oracle→Oracle) or heterogeneous (Oracle→Aurora PostgreSQL) with ongoing replication', 'AWS SCT converts schemas and code: translates Oracle PL/SQL or SQL Server T-SQL to the target engine — DMS moves data, SCT moves logic', 'Minimal-downtime pattern: DMS replicates continuously, then cut over in a short window — the production database stays live during migration', "Exam framing: 'migrate a database to AWS with least downtime' → DMS; 'convert schema from Oracle to Postgres' → DMS + SCT"], 'Migration flow:\n  1. SCT: assess + convert schema/code\n  2. DMS: full load + change-data-capture replication\n  3. Validate: row counts, checksums, app tests\n  4. Cutover: stop writes, final sync, flip connection\n\nAssess, replicate, validate, cut over.', 'DMS replicates data, not everything: large objects, some data types, and secondary indexes need attention — and the source stays the authority until cutover. The exam keeps it simple: DMS = move data, SCT = convert schema.', ['Expecting DMS to rewrite application SQL — that is SCT + dev work', 'Cutting over without validation — silent data gaps', 'One-shot dump/restore when downtime is unacceptable'], 'Migrate Oracle on-prem to Aurora PostgreSQL, <5 min downtime: which services, what order?')], [('clf-db-multiaz', 'Standby or Scale?', 'An e-commerce RDS MySQL primary handles checkout writes fine, but product-page reads saturate it every afternoon. The team proposes enabling Multi-AZ.', 'Will Multi-AZ solve the read saturation?', ['No: Multi-AZ provides a non-readable standby for failover (availability), not read scaling. Add Read Replicas to absorb the read traffic — or both, for availability AND scale.', 'Yes — Multi-AZ adds read capacity.', 'Yes, but only with Aurora.', 'No — switch to DynamoDB.'], 0, 'The correct answer keeps the two features straight: Multi-AZ = standby for failover (you cannot query it); Read Replicas = async readable copies for scaling. The exam tests this distinction relentlessly.', 'Can you run queries against a Multi-AZ standby?', ['Distinguish Multi-AZ from Read Replicas', 'Scale reads with replicas', 'Design for availability and performance'], 10, [['Symptom', 'RDS primary CPU 95% on reads, 14:00-17:00 daily; writes fine']]), ('clf-db-aurora', 'The Global Read Problem', 'A SaaS runs PostgreSQL-compatible RDS in us-east-1. EU users complain about slow dashboards. The team needs fast regional reads with minimal lag.', 'What is the best move?', ['Cache everything in ElastiCache.', 'Aurora Global Database: PostgreSQL-compatible with cross-region read replicas at under a second of lag — built for exactly this global-read pattern.', 'Add RDS Read Replicas in eu-west-1.', 'Migrate to DynamoDB.'], 1, "The correct answer uses Aurora's global design: sub-second cross-region replication beats RDS cross-region read replicas (higher lag), while keeping PostgreSQL compatibility. DynamoDB would force a rewrite; caching alone does not fix the source.", 'Which service offers sub-second global replication for relational data?', ['Apply Aurora Global Database', 'Keep engine compatibility', 'Design for global reads'], 15, [['Complaint', 'EU dashboard p95 latency 2.8s; US p95 300ms; Postgres required']]), ('clf-db-dynamo', 'The Session Store', 'A mobile game needs session storage: 200k writes/sec at peak, single-digit millisecond latency, traffic triples on launch weekends.', 'Which database fits?', ['Redshift.', 'Neptune.', 'DynamoDB On-Demand: serverless key-value at any scale, millisecond latency, and pay-per-request pricing that absorbs the 3x launch spikes without provisioning.', 'RDS MySQL with Read Replicas.'], 2, 'The correct answer matches the workload adjectives to the service: massive key-value throughput + spiky + millisecond = DynamoDB On-Demand. RDS cannot scale writes this way; Redshift is analytics; Neptune is graphs.', 'Which database scales writes serverlessly with millisecond latency?', ['Choose DynamoDB for key-value scale', 'Use On-Demand for spiky traffic', 'Match service to workload adjectives'], 10, [['Requirements', '200k writes/sec peak, <10ms latency, 3x weekend spikes']]), ('clf-db-redshift', 'The Analytics Ask', 'Finance runs complex queries over 5 years of sales data (40 TB) every morning. They tried the production RDS and nearly took down checkout.', 'What is the right architecture?', ['Bigger RDS instance.', 'DynamoDB with DAX.', 'ElastiCache in front of RDS.', 'Redshift: move analytics to a petabyte-scale columnar data warehouse — OLAP belongs off the transactional database, loaded nightly via ETL.'], 3, "The correct answer separates OLTP from OLAP: Redshift's columnar, massively-parallel design exists for exactly these scan-heavy queries, and isolating them protects checkout. Bigger RDS just delays the next incident.", 'What distinguishes OLAP from OLTP workloads?', ['Separate analytics from transactions', 'Choose Redshift for warehousing', 'Protect production with isolation'], 10, [['Incident', 'Morning finance queries saturated RDS; checkout latency spiked 10x']]), ('clf-db-cache', 'The Hot Key', 'Product pages hit the database for the same top-100 items thousands of times per second. RDS CPU is pegged; the data changes hourly.', 'What is the most effective fix?', ['ElastiCache (Redis) in front of RDS: cache the hot items with a 1-hour TTL — microsecond reads absorb the repeated traffic and RDS breathes again.', 'Add 5 Read Replicas.', 'Migrate to Aurora.', 'Increase RDS instance size twice.'], 0, 'The correct answer caches the repeatable reads: identical queries thousands of times per second with hourly changes are the textbook cache case. Replicas and bigger instances scale expensively what caching solves cheaply.', 'When does caching beat scaling the database?', ['Apply ElastiCache for hot reads', 'Set TTLs to data freshness', 'Cache before scaling'], 10, [['Pattern', 'Top-100 items, 3k reads/sec, data changes hourly, RDS CPU 98%']]), ('clf-db-neptune', 'The Fraud Graph', 'A bank needs to find fraud rings: accounts connected through shared devices, addresses, and phone numbers, several hops deep. SQL self-joins are timing out.', 'Which database fits?', ['DocumentDB.', 'Neptune: a managed graph database built for multi-hop relationship queries — traversals that choke relational joins run natively on graph structure.', 'DynamoDB.', 'Redshift.'], 1, "The correct answer matches 'relationship-heavy, multi-hop' to the graph service. Relational joins explode combinatorially on deep traversals; Neptune's graph engine is purpose-built for exactly this.", 'Which workload adjective points to a graph database?', ['Choose Neptune for relationship queries', 'Recognize graph-shaped problems', 'Avoid relational for deep traversals'], 10, [['Query', "'Accounts within 4 hops of flagged device' — SQL times out at 120s"]]), ('clf-db-dms', 'The Zero-Downtime Move', 'A hospital must migrate its Oracle patient database to AWS with near-zero downtime — the system cannot go offline during business hours.', 'Which approach fits?', ['Rewrite the application first.', 'Snowball the database files.', 'DMS with ongoing replication: full load plus continuous change-data capture keeps AWS in sync, then cut over in a brief window — SCT converts the Oracle schema to the target engine.', 'Weekend dump and restore.'], 2, 'The correct answer is the minimal-downtime pattern: DMS replicates continuously so the cutover window shrinks to minutes, and SCT handles Oracle-to-target conversion. Dump/restore means hours of downtime.', 'Which service replicates a database continuously during migration?', ['Use DMS for minimal downtime', 'Pair SCT for heterogeneous moves', 'Plan cutover windows'], 15, [['Constraint', 'Patient system, zero business-hours downtime, Oracle source']]), ('clf-db-docdb', 'The MongoDB Move', "A startup's app is built on MongoDB. They want a managed AWS database without rewriting their queries and drivers.", 'What is the best fit?', ['DynamoDB.', 'Neptune.', 'RDS PostgreSQL with JSONB.', 'DocumentDB: MongoDB-compatible managed document database — existing drivers and queries largely work against the AWS-managed endpoint.'], 3, "The correct answer preserves the existing investment: DocumentDB speaks the MongoDB wire protocol, so migration avoids the rewrite that DynamoDB's different API would demand.", 'Which service offers MongoDB compatibility on AWS?', ['Choose DocumentDB for MongoDB workloads', 'Preserve driver compatibility', 'Avoid rewrites in migrations'], 10, [['Codebase', '200k lines on MongoDB drivers; team wants managed AWS']])], icon='🗄️'),)
    new_domains.append(mock_domain(CN))
    new_domains.append(capstone_domain(CN, 'Cloud Architecture Project', 'a production-ready AWS architecture: multi-AZ compute, managed database, cost model, and a Well-Architected review', ['Design the architecture: VPC, compute, database, and CDN for a 3-tier web app', 'Apply shared responsibility: document what AWS owns vs what you own at each layer', 'Model the cost: pricing calculator estimate with On-Demand vs Savings Plans analysis', 'Secure it: IAM roles, security groups, encryption, and CloudTrail logging', "Review against the Well-Architected Framework's six pillars and remediate gaps"]))
    labs += [LB('lab-clf-pricing', 'Pricing Calculator Estimate', 'Build a cost estimate for a 3-tier app using the AWS Pricing Calculator and compare On-Demand vs Savings Plans.', ['Define the architecture: 4x EC2 t3.medium, 1x RDS db.m5.large Multi-AZ, 100GB S3, CloudFront 500GB/mo', 'Enter it in the AWS Pricing Calculator; record the monthly On-Demand total', 'Apply a 3-year Compute Savings Plan estimate; record the discounted total', 'Write the recommendation: which commitment, break-even, and risks'], [('ls pricing-estimate.md', 'Savings Plan', 'estimate missing — complete the calculator exercise first')], 'Cost estimate complete with commitment analysis. You now price before you build.'), LB('lab-clf-vpc', 'VPC Design on Paper', 'Design a multi-AZ VPC with public/private subnets, route tables, and security group rules.', ['Draw the VPC: /16 CIDR, 2 public + 2 private subnets across 2 AZs', 'Place: ALB in public, EC2 in private, RDS in private DB subnets; IGW + NAT gateway routing', 'Write the security group rules: ALB 443 from 0.0.0.0/0, EC2 from ALB only, RDS 3306 from EC2 only', 'Document the failure test: one AZ goes down — what survives?'], [('ls vpc-design.md', 'security group', 'VPC design missing — draw the architecture first')], 'Multi-AZ VPC designed with least-privilege network rules. Traffic flows, blast radius contained.'), LB('lab-clf-iam', 'IAM Least-Privilege Policy', 'Write and validate an IAM policy granting a deploy role exactly the permissions it needs.', ["Define the role's job: deploy to Elastic Beanstalk, read CloudWatch logs, nothing else", 'Write the JSON policy: scoped actions, specific ARNs, no wildcards on sensitive actions', 'Add MFA condition for console access; attach via a group, not directly to the user', 'Run IAM Access Analyzer findings; document any flagged overly-broad grants'], [('ls deploy-role-policy.json', 'Action', 'IAM policy missing — write the scoped policy first')], 'Least-privilege deploy policy written and analyzed. The role can do its job — and nothing else.'), LB('lab-clf-s3-tiers', 'S3 Storage Class Lifecycle', 'Design an S3 lifecycle policy moving data through storage classes by age and access pattern.', ['Classify the data: hot reports (30d), warm archives (90d), compliance logs (7yr)', 'Map to classes: Standard -> Intelligent-Tiering -> Glacier Flexible -> Deep Archive', 'Write the lifecycle JSON: transitions at 30/90/180 days, expiration for temp data', 'Compute the cost delta vs all-Standard; document retrieval-time tradeoffs'], [('ls s3-lifecycle.json', 'Transition', 'lifecycle policy missing — classify the data first')], 'Lifecycle policy designed with cost math. Data now ages into cheaper storage automatically.')]
    d2, l2 = build_awsclf2()
    new_domains.extend(d2)
    labs.extend(l2)
    return (new_domains, labs)
